WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.380
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Our story begins in an ancient forest

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.100
in the remote town of East Vandermonde.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.190
Colin and Sylwia-- two college students on spring break--

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.440
are hiking through the forest.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.850
COLIN: Why did you want to come all the way here, Sylwia?

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.640
We're in the middle of nowhere.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.730
We should be studying for our probability course.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:47.770
SYLWIA: Perhaps, we are.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.860
PEACOCK: What are you doing?

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.460
These humans won't understand our sacred identities!

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.319
TOUCAN: Patience, my friend.

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:06.740
They will learn.

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.130
COLIN: What is this?

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.130
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Colin and Sylwia don't know what

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:12.910
to make of the scroll they have found.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.270
COLIN: That's impossible.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.810
It looks like they just added the a and b to get a plus b.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.170
And added the k and c minus k to get c.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.430
What kind of bird brain would come up with a formula like this?

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.560
SYLWIA: It looks mysterious and beautiful,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.780
but that hardly means it isn't true.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:33.290
Let's try some examples and see if we can prove or disprove it.

00:01:37.937 --> 00:01:39.770
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Colin remembers from STAT110

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.500
how a binomial coefficient n choose k can

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.760
be defined in terms of factorials, n factorial over n

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.300
minus k factorial k factorial.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:53.360
He tries a couple of examples on his calculator.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.360
It is true in those examples.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:58.360
So he proceeds to work on a proof using algebra.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.512
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.000
COLIN: [SIGHS] It's more of a mystery than ever.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.789
I wonder if we'll ever know if this formula is always true,

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.340
or how anyone could have come up with it.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:18.462
SYLWIA: Actually, I can prove it in a few sentences with no algebra needed.

00:02:18.462 --> 00:02:20.250
COLIN: Huh?

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.510
SYLWIA: Look at the birds.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.190
They will show you.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:26.190
JOE BLITZSTEIN: Sylwia reminds Colin that they learned in STAT110

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:31.120
that the binomial coefficient n choose k can also be defined by a story.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.160
It's the number of ways to choose a committee of size k from n people,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.370
where the order in which people are chosen doesn't matter.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.071
It's the number of subsets of size k for a set of size n.

00:02:43.071 --> 00:02:45.450
It's the number of flocks of k birds that

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.370
can be formed by choosing birds from a flock of n birds

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.710
COLIN: I see peacocks and toucans flying around trying

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.835
to get on the top branches, but what does that have

00:02:54.835 --> 00:02:57.090
to do with the formula on the scroll?

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.120
SYLWIA: Let's say there are a peacocks and b

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.960
toucans vying for the 6 top branches.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.830
The number of possibilities for which birds get to perch on top branches

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:11.580
is a plus b choose 6.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.870
That's the right hand side when c is 6.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.240
COLIN: OK, but what about the left hand side?

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.520
SYLWIA: The left hand side just breaks this into cases.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.210
If there is 1 peacock perched on a top branch,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:26.570
there must be 5 toucans perched on top branches.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:30.320
If there are 2 peacocks, there must be 4 toucans.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:35.450
If there are k peacocks, there must be c minus k toucans.

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:37.880
So count how many possibilities there are

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:43.310
for which k peacocks and which c minus k toucans are on top branches.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.240
Then sum up the cases since we want the total number of possibilities,

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.702
not just the number for one value of k.

00:03:49.702 --> 00:03:51.410
JOE BLITZSTEIN: The formula on the scroll

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.900
is called Vandermonde's Identity.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.590
Sylwia has just discovered a story proof of this identity.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.410
She has given it an interpretation rather than doing

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:03.080
tedious or intractable algebra.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.460
Both sides of the identity count the same thing, so they must be equal.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:10.380
Sylwia gave her proof for the case c equal to 6

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.560
because she was looking at a tree with 6 top branches.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.720
But the same idea works for any value of c.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.950
Vandermonde's Identity is a handy result that

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:22.940
often comes up in problems involving binomial coefficients.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.460
Thinking about it in terms of a story makes it easier

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.400
to remember and understand.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.600
The moral of the story is to look for a story.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:35.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

